+            $display("J9_PROBE2 pc=%08x instr_assembled=%08x cross_assemble=%0b residue=%04x cur_half_lo=%04x cur_half_hi=%04x is_comp_lo=%0b is_comp_hi=%0b at_cross_boundary=%0b upcoming_cross=%0b consecutive_cross=%0b i_mem_rdata=%08x i_mem_addr=%08x if_ex_pc=%08x if_ex_instr=%08x id_illegal=%0b",
+                     dut.if_pc,
+                     dut.instr_assembled,
+                     dut.cross_assemble,
+                     dut.residue,
+                     dut.cur_half_lo,
+                     dut.cur_half_hi,
+                     dut.is_comp_lo,
+                     dut.is_comp_hi,
+                     dut.at_cross_boundary,
+                     dut.upcoming_cross,
+                     dut.consecutive_cross,
+                     i_mem_rdata,
+                     i_mem_addr,
+                     dut.if_ex_pc,
+                     dut.if_ex_instr,
+                     dut.id_illegal);
+        end
+
         watchdog <= watchdog + 1;
         if (watchdog > max_cycles) begin
             $display("FAIL: watchdog timeout commits=%0d pc=%08x", commit_count, dbg_pc);

codex
The second probe shows `pc=0x12` is assembled correctly (`000f8067`) and `id_illegal=0` while it is in IF. The trap therefore comes later in the pipe, so I’m narrowing the probe to the `if_ex/ex_mem/ex_wb` illegal bits for that same PC before making another RTL change.
